<td id="S5.T4.7.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">57.2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57.2\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.7.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.7.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T4.7.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Yes-or-no</span></th>
<td id="S5.T4.7.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">57.5</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57.5\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.7.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="58.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">58.0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">58.0\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.7.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">59.9</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.9\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.7.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">56.5</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56.5\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.7.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">57.0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57.0\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Investigating content and quality retrieval.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Evaluation on VLMs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ R4-CGQA: Retrieval-based Vision Language Models for Computer Graphics Image Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, “w/o. quality” means removing the quality embedding module during similarity search, and “w/o. quality” denotes using REIQA rather than CLIP for content embedding and the quality embedding module is also removed.
For Llava-7B, the proposed full pipeline further improves the Choice accuracy to
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>59.8</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> and keeps a high Yes-or-no accuracy (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>59.9</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>), yielding absolute
gains of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+9.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>9.7</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+9.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+11.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11.1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+11.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> over the Base model.
For Llama3.2-V, the same trend holds: quality-only retrieval is already
strong on Yes-or-no questions, but the full framework still achieves the best overall
results (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>66.7</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="69.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>69.0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">69.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> on Choice / Yes-or-no), with a large gain of
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+13.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>13.2</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+13.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> on Yes-or-no accuracy. Besides, all of full pipelines achieve highest <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\&amp;A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">&amp;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\&amp;A</annotation></semantics></math> scores.
These results confirm that the content and quality based retrieval is more robust than using either branch alone.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Investigating multi-image input.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural question is why we do not simply input multiple CG images into the VLM at the same time.
In general, conventional VLMs do not perform robust comparative analysis over multiple images, so feeding multiple images often leads to a significant decline in performance. Nevertheless, we also conduct an experiment on Pixtral, which is capable of processing multiple images, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Evaluation on VLMs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ R4-CGQA: Retrieval-based Vision Language Models for Computer Graphics Image Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The results show that when we input similar images together with the current image (Multi-image (only)), performance on the Choice metric decreases by <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.3</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Even when multiple images are combined with our method (Multi-image + R4-CGQA), performance on Choice is still <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower than R4-CGQA alone. The results suggest that simply feeding multiple images into VLMs is not a reasonable solution for CGQA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Investigating <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table  <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2 Evaluation on VLMs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ R4-CGQA: Retrieval-based Vision Language Models for Computer Graphics Image Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the effect of the candidate set size <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> for
Llava-7B with a fixed similarity threshold <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>.
Accuracy improves when increasing <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> (up to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>59.8</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> /
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>59.9</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> on Choice / Yes-or-no), but drops again when <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is further
enlarged, indicating that a moderate number of neighbours is preferable to
either a single example or an overly large, noisy candidate set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Investigating <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure  <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Investigating 𝑇. ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ R4-CGQA: Retrieval-based Vision Language Models for Computer Graphics Image Quality Assessment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows the impact of the threshold <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for
LlavaNext-8B and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\{5,7\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\{5,7\}</annotation></semantics></math>: accuracies are relatively stable when
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> lies between <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, and the configuration with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=5</annotation></semantics></math> is
consistently more stable than <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=7</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., do not choose similar image descriptions as examples, the performance decline drastically on Yes-or-no.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10578v1/accuracy_vs_T_yesno.png" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/215;" width="685" height="215" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S5.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The influence of threshold <math id="S5.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and the accuracy rate of responses was tested on LlavaNext-8B, and we investigate <math id="S5.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=7</annotation></semantics></math>.  </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address the limitations of current CG quality assessment by introducing a new dataset of 3.5K CG images with detailed text-based quality descriptions covering six perceptual dimensions. Building upon this resource, we further propose a retrieval-augmented, two-stream framework that leverages both content similarity and quality similarity to supply VLMs with relevant example descriptions during inference, named R4-CGQA. Extensive experiments on several representative VLMs demonstrate that the retrieved textual cues substantially enhance their ability to provide accurate and interpretable CG quality judgments. Our contributions offer a scalable and training-free solution for empowering VLMs in CGQA, which can strongly support future research in this direction.</p>
</div>
</section>
